// EXPECT: E0237 — an enum member's value that needs evaluation (ADR-0041 §3, ADR-0129 §2)
//
// **This file's construct changed with ADR-0129**, the same way `023-array-length-not-literal` changed
// with ADR-0070 and for the same reason. It used to be `COUNT :: 3;` with `X :: COUNT;`, which is now
// *legal*: for a constant whose initialiser is already an integer literal there is nothing to evaluate, so
// sema resolves the name against the file scope it already consults and reads the literal out of the HIR.
// No `jr-db`, no `jr-vm`, no inversion of ADR-0018 §3's phase order — `jr-sema` still depends on neither.
//
// The asymmetry that closed was recorded by ADR-0127 §3 as a promise a completed wave had not kept, and
// E0237's own note admitted it: an array length had learnt this trick and an enum member had not.
//
// What remains refused is a value that needs an actual **evaluation**, which is where ADR-0041 §3's
// argument still holds exactly as written. Folding `SUM` here would mean a *second* constant folder beside
// ADR-0022's, which is the duplication ADR-0018 §2 refuses for layout and ADR-0020 §2 for trap messages.
//
// **This file pins the wording for a value that is not a name at all.** Its pair,
// `074-enum-member-not-a-usable-constant`, pins the other branch — a value that *is* a name but not a
// usable one. ADR-0070 §3's rule is why there are two: a reader who named something and a reader who wrote
// arithmetic need different advice, and one message cannot be true for both.
COUNT :: 3;

Bad :: enum {
    // Legal now, and here so the file shows the line rather than only one side of it.
    OK :: COUNT;

    // E0237: arithmetic needs the evaluator, which runs after an enum's members are typed.
    X :: 2 + 2;
}

main :: () {
}
